//! flags: -p ssa

// The SSA side of correctness/llvm/while-loops.bonsai: what a loop with no
// stated trip count becomes on the way through SSA and back.
//
// Three things to look for in the golden, each of which was broken.
//
// A `while` reached the SSA builder as a TODO and stopped the compiler. It is
// three blocks -- a header holding the test, a body, and what comes after --
// and the test lives in the header because it is asked again every time round.
//
// A loop carries values, and the back edge has to hand them on. The builder
// gives a block an argument when something inside it reads a name the block
// does not define, and threads the value in from the predecessors it has --
// which, while the loop is being built, is only the block before it. Closing
// the cycle means passing each of those on as the body left it, and the back
// edge used to pass nothing at all, which failed as a bad argument count.
//
// A `do ... while` with no branch in its body is one block that jumps to
// itself: its own header and its own latch. The relooper wraps everything
// emitted since the region began into the body, so a block that is its own
// header has to start a region -- otherwise `total` and its initialiser, which
// come before the loop, end up inside it and are redone on every iteration.
func [[export]] countdown(n : u32) -> u32 {
    i : mut u32 = n;
    steps : mut u32 = 0u;
    while i > 0u {
        i = i - 1u;
        steps = steps + 1u;
    }
    return steps;
}

func [[export]] doubling(n : u32) -> u32 {
    total : mut u32 = 1u;
    do {
        total = total * 2u;
    } while total < n;
    return total;
}
